                    {
                        id delete-libc-calls
                        title "The 155 libc calls that std, core or bstr already do, per docs/libc-surface.md"
                        priority 0
                        kind @refactor
                        deps (delete-memalloc)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-09T18:56:01.001624416Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "This node exists because 394 direct `libc::` call sites had no owner, and `libc::putenv` surviving into a crate that has `std::env` is what exposed it — surviving while holding a pointer into shell-owned storage that glibc does not copy, which is a live use-after-free.\n\n`docs/std-replacements.md` asks which *reimplementations* std already does: `mystring.rs`, `output.rs`'s `vsnprintf`, the hash tables. It lists direct libc calls as *riders* — \"what to delete while you are already in there\". That framing is the whole reason nothing got done: a rider is only done if someone is already in that file for another purpose, and no node's title sends anyone to `histedit.rs` or `bltin/test.rs`. Eight sites in each, and both files are untouched by every node in the plan.\n\n`docs/libc-surface.md` is the measurement, symbol by symbol. 100 distinct symbols, 394 sites, four classes:\n\n* **A, 116 sites in 19 symbols** — `<[u8]>`/`bstr`/`core::ptr` one-liners. `strlen` 39, `strchr` 17, `strcmp` 15, `strcpy` 7, `strspn` 5, `memmove` 5, `memcpy` 5, `strpbrk` 4, and eleven more. The compiler decides these, but only once the operand is an owned value — converting the call while it is still `*mut c_char` buys a cast, not a deletion. That is why this node deps on [[delete-memalloc]] rather than running beside it.\n* **B, 39 sites in 12 symbols** — std has it and one local argument settles it: the last raw `free`/`malloc`/`realloc`, `putenv`, `getopt`, `getcwd`, `chdir`, `abort`, `snprintf`, and the stdio four that ride [[output-is-a-writer]].\n* **C, 18 sites in 8 symbols** — traps. `isalpha`/`isalnum`/`isspace`/`isdigit`, `strcoll`, `atoi`, `strtod`, `fnmatch`, `strerror`, `strsignal`, the three `stat64` variants. std has each and the behaviour differs; `docs/std-replacements.md` §5 measures why. None may be swapped without either showing the difference unobservable or registering it.\n* **D, 221 sites in 61 symbols** — std does not have it, and these stay. `fork`, `vfork`, `execve`, `waitpid`, the nine signal calls, `tcsetpgrp`, `sigprocmask`, `setpgid`, `getrlimit`, `memfd_create`. Recording the class *is* the deliverable: a reader who does not find them listed as deliberately kept will spend an afternoon proposing `std::process::Command`.\n\nSo the honest headline is that most of this crate's libc surface is correct — it is a POSIX shell and a shell that stopped calling `fork` would not be one. 155 of 394 are not.\n\nScheduling: class A is not a rider, it is the second half of [dec:nsh:owned-data] seen from the call site rather than the allocation, and it goes per file alongside the container work rather than after it. Class B's `putenv` is a defect, not a cleanup, and does not wait for this node."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-12T08:43:23.424730657Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-12T10:14:46.834486194Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @decision
                                text "Closed. Classes A and B of `docs/libc-surface.md` are at zero except four sites, and §7 of that document records the outcome symbol by symbol.\n\nOf the 155 sites the node was scoped to, 47 had already gone as consequences of the nodes it waited on — [[delete-memalloc]] took the `free`/`malloc`/`realloc` and the `strspn`/`strdup`/`bsearch` group, [[output-is-a-writer]] took the stdio four and `snprintf`, [[owned-vars]] took `putenv` (the use-after-free in §5, which is fixed), and the builtin restructure took `getcwd` and `strtok`. This node converted the remaining 104 in seven commits, one per concern: comparisons, byte scans, block moves, lengths (twice, to stay inside the WIP budget), errno, and `abort`/`chdir`.\n\n**Four sites remain and all four are outside the node's title**, which is what std, core or bstr already do:\n\n* `input.rs` `set_errno` and `mystring.rs` `atomax` write errno. std reports the last OS error and cannot assign one.\n* `shellmain.rs` `main` wants errno's *address*, which std does not expose at all. It is `main.c`'s cache of the TLS lookup and is ported for the pointer.\n* `builtins/fc.rs` `scan_options` calls `getopt(3)`. std has no `getopt`, so this is a rewrite and not a replacement — and what is wrong with it is that `optind` is a process global and the scan permutes `fc`'s array, which is [[no-ambient-state]]'s question. The note already in that file said as much before this node started, and it was right.\n\nThree conversions are deliberately not the obvious one, each documented where it stands: `pmatch` keeps a byte-by-byte `strncmp` (`mystring::ncmp_eq`) because the C relies on the early stop at a shared NUL to read past a single stack byte only as far as the terminator; `argstr`'s `strcspn` and `pmatch`'s two scans count to the first stop byte rather than take `CStr`'s whole remaining string, because both re-enter per control byte; and three `strchr` sites keep the terminator *in* the set, because `strchr` matches it — that is how `read` escapes a NUL from the input, how a NUL counts as an IFS separator, and what `umask` does at the end of a mode.\n\n**Gates.** `runall.sh 6` PASS=61514 FAIL=0 FLAKY=40 XFAIL=4, no stale register entries; divtest PASS=44 FAIL=0; ptydiff PASS=37 FAIL=0; POSIX cases PASS=660 FAIL=51. Every one is the baseline exactly. `cargo test -p nsh` 160 + 11, green.\n\n**Unsafe ops: 3829 → 3779**, and the small size of that number is the honest result rather than a disappointment. `CStr::from_ptr` is itself an unsafe call, so converting a `strlen` on a `*const c_char` trades one unsafe call for another; what the metric moves on is the errno consolidation, where `system::errno` is safe and four `unsafe fn` wrappers and their dereferences went with it. `docs/libc-surface.md` §2 predicted this — the call site is downstream of [dec:nsh:owned-data], and converting it while the operand is still a raw pointer buys a cast rather than a deletion. The rest of the drop is [[move-state]]'s and whatever follows it.\n\nAlso recorded: a review of this node's diff widened to its neighbours and found seven defects in [[printf-builtin]], two reproduced, one of which panics the shell. Filed as errata there rather than fixed here, because this node's oracle is that nothing changes."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-12T10:14:51.965438121Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }
                )
            }

