            {
                id sorted-tables
                title "env and alias print sorted: BTreeMap tables, and the register's first entry"
                priority 0
                kind @fix
                deps (sanctioned-divergences)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:44:42.574884968Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-08T11:57:04.358106034Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The title named four builtins and two of them were wrong. `export -p` and a bare `set` both print through `showvars`, which already `qsort`s with `vpcmp` before printing, so both shells printed those sorted before this change and print them sorted after it. Only `env` -- anything that reads `environ`, since `listvars` builds `execve`'s `envp` -- and a bare `alias` change. Measured on both binaries before touching anything. The register entry is scoped to `env`, `printenv` and `alias` for the same reason: naming `export -p` or `set` would let it excuse a permutation that could only be a regression.\n\nThe corpus observes it once, not thirty times: `aud_state_var.txt`'s `export XV=1 YV=2; echo \"$XV$YV\"; env | grep -E '^(XV|YV)='`. The forty-odd `env` cases in `aud_state_gen3` are all `env | grep -c '^NAME='`, which counts rather than lists, and the `alias` cases print one alias or none.\n\nFive C symbols lost their target counterpart -- `hashval`, `hashvar`, `varcmp`, `varequal`, `vpcmp` -- since all five existed only to serve the hash table. Retired with the transaction `delete-gen` established: marker lines out of `docs/spec/port/src/var.md` with every word of prose kept, entries out of `plan/annotations.styx` and `plan/.port-manifest.styx`. Spec coverage 460/460 to 455/455."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T16:24:49.364256759Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Done. Both halves landed and both are exercised on every sweep.\n\nThe tables: `var.rs` and `alias.rs` hold `BTreeMap`s, so `env`, `printenv` and `alias` print in name order where dash prints in the order its 39 hash buckets happen to chain. `export -p` and `set` are deliberately untouched — dash already `qsort`s those in `showvars` (`src/var.c:409`), so both shells print them sorted and always did.\n\nThe register: `dsdiv_sorted_tables` in `tests/harness/divergences.sh` is the first entry, and it is the reason the sweep can still report FAIL=0. It asserts five things rather than ignoring the difference — matching exit status, a case that actually runs `env`/`printenv`/`alias`, an identical multiset of lines, only assignment-shaped lines having moved, and the port's blocks being the sorted side. The last two are what keep it from excusing a regression: `ds_same_lines` alone would excuse every permutation, including one that is neither shell's.\n\nCurrent state on master: `runall.sh 8` reports XFAIL=1 with no STALE (the entry fires in `aud_state_var`), and `divtest.sh` reports PASS=28 FAIL=0 — twenty-eight tests that all assert *refusals*, because the way this entry fails is by matching too much.\n\nKnown limit, pinned by `divtest.sh` so it cannot drift silently: `ds_blocks_sorted` reads each maximal run of assignment-shaped lines as one block, so a case printing two environments back to back with nothing between them would be refused and reported as a failure. Nothing in `tests/corpus` does, and a loud refusal is the right way for this entry to be wrong."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-09T16:24:56.014094132Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
